{"name":"Cement Feet","hex":"#7b737b","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cement","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A powdered substance produced by firing (calcining) calcium carbonate (limestone) and clay that develops strong cohesive properties when mixed with water. The main ingredient of concrete.","The paste-like substance resulting from mixing such a powder with water, or the rock-like substance that forms when it dries."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To affix with cement.","To overlay or coat with cement."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cement"},{"term":"feet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of foot"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Fact; performance; feat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/feet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cement","blocks":[{"text":"A cement is a binder used for construction that sets, hardens, and adheres to other materials to bind them together. Cement is seldom used on its own, but rather to bind sand and gravel (aggregate) together. Cement mixed with fine aggregate produces mortar for masonry, or with sand and gravel, produces concrete. Concrete is the most widely used material in existence and is behind only water as the planet's most-consumed resource."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/USMC-110806-M-IX060-148.jpg/330px-USMC-110806-M-IX060-148.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cement","wikidata":"Q45190"},{"title":"Cement shoes","blocks":[{"text":"Cement shoes, concrete shoes, or Chicago overcoat is a method of murder or body disposal, usually associated with criminals such as the Mafia or gangs. It involves placing the victim's feet into wet cement or concrete, allowing it time to set, and then throwing them into water in the hope the body will never be found. In the US, the term has become a tongue-in-cheek euphemism for a threat of death by criminals. It is a common trope in fiction."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cement_shoes","wikidata":"Q5058656"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/USMC-110806-M-IX060-148.jpg/500px-USMC-110806-M-IX060-148.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/USMC-110806-M-IX060-148.jpg/1280px-USMC-110806-M-IX060-148.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/USMC-110806-M-IX060-148.jpg/500px-USMC-110806-M-IX060-148.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cement powder being poured from a bag, ready to be mixed with aggregates and water.[1]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUSMC-110806-M-IX060-148.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/LDClinkerScaled.jpg/500px-LDClinkerScaled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/LDClinkerScaled.jpg/500px-LDClinkerScaled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Clinker nodules produced by sintering at 1450 °C","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALDClinkerScaled.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Calcium_oxide_powder.JPG/500px-Calcium_oxide_powder.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Calcium_oxide_powder.JPG/1280px-Calcium_oxide_powder.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Calcium_oxide_powder.JPG/500px-Calcium_oxide_powder.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Calcium oxide obtaine